\newlabel{fig:strong}{{2}{6}{\textbf {States decompose where the system's own outputs stay weak.} \textbf {a.} Trained on 8 glyphs, an MLP renders wholes of new glyphs poorly (orange frames), while a linear probe of its state recovers them (blue frames); trained on 64 glyphs, it does both. Numbers give $R^2$ to the target. \textbf {b.} Own decoders reproduce new pairs of training glyphs (dashed, open) but fail on new glyphs at small $V$ (solid), while a probe of the state stays high ($m = 64$). \textbf {c.} A recursive composer's own decomposer fails beyond its training depths (shaded), where a probe of the same root state follows the bound of Proposition~\ref {prop:depth} (dotted). \textbf {d.} Image--text encoders match captions with swapped places at chance, while their quadrant states recover both photographs in their fixed places. Means over 3--9 seeds, with standard errors.\relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{sec:diffusion}{{6.4}{6}{}{subsection.6.4}{}}
\newlabel{sec:behavior}{{6.5}{6}{}{subsection.6.5}{}}
\citation{fodor1988}
\newlabel{fig:both}{{3}{7}{\textbf {Where a model answers by meaning, the answer runs through a recoverable question state.} \textbf {a.} Answers (orange), the figure probe of the question state (blue) and of the average over tokens (grey) for two pairs of new nouns; bold marks the figure and red the ground. \textbf {b.} Answer accuracy against the role transfer of the question state for seven language models on the main prompt (large dots) and three others (small dots); models that answer by word order sit at the lower left (red). \textbf {c.} Patching the whole question state from a source with the opposite figure flips the answer from the later layers on. \textbf {d.} Patching a subspace at each model's selected layer (phi-4, Qwen3-8B, OLMo-2 13B): the top directions of the source--target difference flip more answers than the figure probe or the top principal components, and random directions flip almost none. Means over 3 seeds, and 2 in \textbf {d}.\relax }{figure.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{sec:origins}{{6.6}{7}{}{subsection.6.6}{}}
