\newlabel{tab:objective}{{2}{20}{\textbf {A recovery objective over many parts improves the state and the network's own decoder, whereas one over training parts leaves the state unchanged.} Values are $R^2$ on new glyphs at $V = 8$, averaged over MLP, CNN and role--filler networks with 5 seeds. The fixed renderer draws the glyphs that the linear head predicts.\relax }{table.caption.10}{}}
\newlabel{app:setup}{{D}{20}{AI Use Statement}{appendix.D}{}}
\newlabel{app:calibration}{{D.2}{20}{AI Use Statement}{subsection.D.2}{}}
\newlabel{tab:capabilities}{{3}{21}{\textbf {The strong form supports every weak capability and adds those that need decomposition.} Each row names a capability, the weakest set of the spectrum that supports it in our tests and the test of this paper that scores it on new parts. Five sets order the spectrum: a blend that keeps no decomposition, then weak, strong, shared role codes and explicit copies (Section~\ref {sec:sets}). Shared role codes give each part one vector per role (Section~\ref {sec:mechanics}), so a written vector or an edit acts on one role alone. No system we tested reaches explicit copies.\relax }{table.caption.11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:measure}{{6}{21}{\textbf {Training parts hide blends, and new parts expose them.} \textbf {a.} Given the candidates, a lookup over wholes built from 32 training parts (open orange) identifies the role-1 part of every operator except the symmetric blend, while a linear probe separates the operators on new parts (filled; red marks the blend $x + y$). \textbf {b.} Linear recovery of new parts meets the ceiling of Proposition~\ref {prop:ceiling} across operators. Means over 5 seeds.\relax }{figure.caption.12}{}}
