\newlabel{tab:systems}{{4}{22}{\textbf {Every system supplies parts, wholes and a state, and each supplies an output of its own.} Parts new to the system never entered its training, whereas parts new to the probe entered pretraining while staying out of the probe's fit. Invented words extend the language models' test to parts new to the model itself.\relax }{table.caption.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:protocol}{{7}{23}{\textbf {One protocol reads the same new parts through three procedures.} \textbf {a.} We split the parts before fitting anything (1). The system encodes wholes of two parts (2, 3), and linear probes fitted on the probe's training parts return each role's part from the state, with error $\varepsilon $ on new parts (4). The same steps, run on the concatenation and on the sum of the same part vectors, calibrate $\varepsilon $ into recoverability $\kappa $ (5). \textbf {b.} A probe trained on many parts measures the representation, a probe trained on the system's training parts stands in for a typical probe, and the system's own output measures behavior. \textbf {c.} A converse paraphrase keeps the figure and reverses the order of mention. A probe of the figure fitted on one phrasing therefore returns the tulip on the other if roles follow meaning, and the barrel if they follow word order. Colors keep one meaning in every figure. Orange marks the system's own output and blue a probe of its state. Green marks a procedure that reads through the state, such as a new decoder, an edit or a patch, grey marks controls and references, and red marks a blend or an answer by word order. Filled markers denote new parts and open markers training parts.\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
