\newlabel{fig:decomp}{{8}{25}{\textbf {Procedures that read through role codes reuse the parts.} Role maps $h \approx c + B_1 e(x) + B_2 e(y)$, fitted on many glyphs, predict and edit states that each network's own decoder renders (\textsc {over}, $V = 64$). \textbf {a.} A code swap replaces the role-1 vector with a donor's vector, and the decoder renders the donor in role 1 while keeping the role-2 glyph. The natural direction $h(a', b') - h(a, b)$, scaled to the same norm, lands far from the target. Numbers give $R^2$ to the target. \textbf {b.} Writing a new glyph's vector through $B_2$ keeps old pairs (dark) and renders the new glyph in a role it never filled (light), with no gradient step, whereas fine-tuning damages both and replay needs stored data. \textbf {c.} A role-1 swap changes the role-2 half of the output less than other changes of the state do. \textbf {d.} In language models that answer by meaning, the state at two role questions places a sentence's converse paraphrase nearer than both role reversals, with no fitted probe, whereas the average over tokens never does; chance is $1/3$ (dotted). Means over 3--5 seeds, with standard errors.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
