\newlabel{fig:weak}{{9}{28}{\textbf {Behavior can outrun a linear probe, and sentence states fail both tests.} \textbf {a.} OLMo-2 7B answers by meaning (orange), and its question state carries the answer under patching (green), while ridge, kernel and MLP probes of that state miss the role (blue), even with hyperparameters chosen on the test set (grey). \textbf {b.} Pythia-1.4b and GPT-2 large answer with the last-mentioned noun, and probes of their question states return the first-mentioned noun. Bold marks the figure and red the ground. \textbf {c.} A figure probe of the average over tokens returns the noun in the figure's word position (red) in every language model and for every probe, while the same probe at the question returns the figure (blue). \textbf {d.} Text encoders tell a sentence's nouns from others (grey), yet they prefer a sentence's converse paraphrase over the same paraphrase with the nouns exchanged on only 0.02--0.15 of pairs (orange), where matching meaning scores 1. New nouns; means over 3 seeds.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\newlabel{tab:remedies}{{5}{29}{\textbf {Reading through the state repairs the failing procedures of glyph networks and recursive composers, and gives language models a sentence embedding that carries the role.} Values are $R^2$ of the system's output for the glyph and recursive networks, and the share of pairs of new nouns whose converse paraphrase is nearer than both role reversals for the language models. Each row compares runs within one experiment. Glyph values average MLP, CNN and role--filler networks over 5--9 seeds, and the sequence of 32 glyphs starts from networks trained on 16 glyphs. Recursive values use width 256, 5 seeds and 20{,}000 training steps, which give 0.27 at depth 3 against 0.26 after 6{,}000 steps in the text, and language-model values use 3 seeds.\relax }{table.caption.17}{}}
