\newlabel{fig:origins}{{10}{30}{\textbf {The input supplies recoverability in the glyph networks, whereas pretrained models build it beyond their inputs.} Each row compares a trained state (blue) with its input (grey) and with random weights (light grey). \textbf {a.} Probes of new glyphs at $V = 8$, on plain inputs and on inputs entangled by a fixed random map. \textbf {b.} Language models against the average and the concatenation of the two nouns' input embeddings. \textbf {c.} Image encoders against raw pixels, scored with each encoder's own part vectors. \textbf {d.} Text encoders. \textbf {e.} Pythia models of growing size, whose scale trend is Pythia's alone, and four other language models (squares). \textbf {f.} Training of Pythia-1.4b, where step 0 is the initialization, and of Pythia-410m from six random seeds (line and range). Probes in \textbf {b} and \textbf {d}--\textbf {f} recover the nouns in their order of mention. $\kappa $ is 1 for the concatenation of the part vectors (dotted) and 0 for their sum. Means over 3--5 seeds.\relax }{figure.caption.18}{}}
